depth-pressure-build-question = Into a beaker of { $name } (12 cm of it) we lower a funnel covered with a rubber film; a tube leads from it to a U-tube manometer with water. We write down how far apart the water levels in the manometer are with the film 3, 6 and 9 cm deep, and at last turn it sideways at 9 cm. How does the liquid’s pressure depend on the depth?
depth-pressure-answer-why = A liquid is heavy: each layer presses on the layers below with the weight of all that is above it. At a depth h each square metre carries a column of liquid h high with a mass ρ·h, that is, a weight ρ·g·h — so the pressure grows exactly in proportion to the depth. And the bits of a liquid slide past each other easily and pass that pressure on in all directions alike (Pascal’s law): the film turned sideways feels the same pressure.
depth-pressure-answer-say = Twice as deep — twice the pressure!
depth-pressure-answer-say-2 = Let us look at the manometer.
depth-pressure-answer-text = {" "}The film turned sideways at { $h } gave the same Δh = { $dh }: at one depth the pressure is the same in all directions.
depth-pressure-answer-text-2 = { $l }. The pressure the manometer shows (p = ρ_water·g·Δh) grew in proportion to the depth: p / h ≈ { $mean } Pa/m everywhere. From it the liquid’s density ρ = p / (g·h) ≈ { $rho } kg/m³ (the table gives { $table } kg/m³).{ $h }
